}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314992 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186314993 :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)+\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(24\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)+\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(24\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186314994 :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186314995 :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(18\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(18\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186314996 :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\right]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."] [:db/cas 17592186315000 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-sa}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>0, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of 