\nf(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯,\\,g(x)=u(x+4)+u(x-6),\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+⋯,\\,g(x)=u(x+4)+u(x-6),\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function."] [:db/cas 17592186317162 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function."] [:db/cas 17592186317162 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in \\left[0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186317163 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-5)\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function." "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+⋯,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-5)\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function."] [:db/cas 17592186317163 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in \\left[0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186317164 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =1-P(X>1) \\\\  & =1-0.0618 \\\\  & =0.9382\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.9382 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.9382 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.54)=0.9382\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)=1.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =1-P(X>1) \\\\  & =1-0.0618 \\\\  & =0.9382\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.9382 \\\\ P\\,\\left(Z\\le \\frac{1-(-1)}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.9382 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.9382 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.54)=0.9382\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)=1.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186317165 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,\\left(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317173 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-3(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{5}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{5}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(4,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{5}+3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left({y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-3(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{5}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{5}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(4,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{5}+3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317174 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-16{y}^{-1/3}+13) \\\\  & =-16\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(13) \\\\  & =-16(-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-4/3})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{-4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}{16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)} & =\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{4}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(16)}{16} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-16{y}^{-1/3}+13) \\\\  & =-16\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(13) \\\\  & =-16\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-4/3}\\right)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{-4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}{16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)} & =\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{4}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(16)}{16} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317175 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2y\\cdot {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){y}^{-3/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6{y}^{-3/2}-3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{y\\sqrt{y}}-3\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3{y}^{2}}{y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y\\sqrt{y}}{6-3{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(-14,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{1}}{6-3(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2y\\cdot {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){y}^{-3/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6{y}^{-3/2}-3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{y\\sqrt{y}}-3\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3{y}^{2}}{y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y\\sqrt{y}}{6-3{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(-14,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{1}}{6-3(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317176 :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2$nd cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=3$ and $f(x)=F(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2$nd cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=3$ and $f(x)=F(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317177 :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3$rd sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={x}^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3$rd sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f\\left(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={x}^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317178 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2+\\frac{5}{2}+1)-((-2)+\\frac{5}{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+x\\right]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(2+\\frac{5}{2}+1\\right)-\\left((-2)+\\frac{5}{2}+(-1)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317179 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317180 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-4$ for $x\\in [-5,5).$ The Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-4$ for $x\\in [-5,5).$ The Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317181 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\in [-7,7).$ The Fourier series of the $14$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\in [-7,7).$ The Fourier series of the $14$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317182 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ The Fourier series of the $16$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ The Fourier series of the $16$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317182 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=8$ and $f(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-8}^{8}(-x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+5x]}_{-8}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}((-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+5\\cdot 8)-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8)^{2}+5\\cdot (-8))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8-(-72)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=8$ and $f(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-8}^{8}(-x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+5x\\right]}_{-8}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+5\\cdot 8\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8)^{2}+5\\cdot (-8)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8-(-72)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317184 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317184 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}(uv\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x))(-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-\\frac{20}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }((-18)(-1)-(22)(-1))-\\frac{80}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\cdot 40) \\\\  & =-\\frac{40}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}(uv\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{\\left[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\right]}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\left(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\right)(-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{\\left[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\right]}_{-4}^{4}-\\frac{20}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }((-18)(-1)-(22)(-1))-\\frac{80}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\cdot 40\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{40}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317186 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $6\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{4}.$\n\n${a}_{4}=$ {{field-1}}" "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n}{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n}{3}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $6\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{4}.$\n\n${a}_{4}=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317186 :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3\\pi $ and $f(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\frac{3}{4}{[(-3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\frac{3}{4}[(-9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi )]+\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0+\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{27}{16}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{16\\pi }(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3\\pi $ and $f(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3\\pi }x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }\\left(\\frac{3}{4}{\\left[(-3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\right]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }\\left(\\frac{3}{4}[(-9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi )]+\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0+\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right){\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right)\\right]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left(-\\frac{27}{16}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\pi )]\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{16\\pi }(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317189 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x-5\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x\\right){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4)\\right)\\right]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317190 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.8289 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.95)=0.8289\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.95 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.95} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.105\\,26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.105\\,26)^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P\\,\\left(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.8289 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.95)=0.8289\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.95 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.95} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.105\\,26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.105\\,26)^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186317194 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3))]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-(-\\frac{9}{2}-12)]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{7}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{33}{2}\\right)]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x\\right){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\left[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-\\left(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3)\\right)\\right]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}-12\\right)\\right]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{7}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{33}{2}\\right)\\right]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317195 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}(\\frac{1}{2}x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4))]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x\\right){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4)\\right)\\right]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317196 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}\\right)\\right]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317200 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-\\frac{6}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-6{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2(1)-6(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+6{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{6}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}+6}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2(3)^{2}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(2y-\\frac{6}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-6{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2(1)-6(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+6{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{6}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}+6}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2(3)^{2}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317201 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-5{y}^{-1}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5(-{y}^{-2})+2y \\\\  & =2y+5{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{3}+5}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{3}+5}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (x-(-4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{4}{7}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-5{y}^{-1}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5(-{y}^{-2})+2y \\\\  & =2y+5{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{3}+5}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{3}+5}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (x-(-4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{4}{7}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317203 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186317210 :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},2\\right).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317210 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},2\\right):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317212 :question/worked-solution "Let $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.\n$$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.\n$$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317214 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}\\right).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317214 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16\\left(y-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =16\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317217 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ and $y=-1$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2} & =4p(-3) \\\\ 1 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ and $y=-1$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2} & =4p(-3) \\\\ 1 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}x\\right) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317221 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1+9}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+3\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1+9}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317239 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+[2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}]-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\right)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-2(1)(1)^{2}}{6(1)^{2}+2(1)^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\left[2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right]-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\right)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-2(1)(1)^{2}}{6(1)^{2}+2(1)^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317240 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$" "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+\\left(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317244 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(6,-2).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 4 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(6,-2).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 4 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}x\\right) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317248 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,2).$" "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317250 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩,\\,0\\le s<\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right\\rangle ,\\,0\\le s<\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317250 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317252 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0)+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,\\frac{11}{2}).$" "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0)+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $\\left(0,\\frac{11}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186317268 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317268 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}){\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\right)}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\right){\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317284 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317284 :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1\\right).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1\\right)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317285 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186317285 :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left({f}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317314 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-{4}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-{4}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317316 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)] \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right] \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317319 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x+1}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3+1}+2(3)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+6) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-25\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}+2x\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3+1}+2(3)\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}+6\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-25\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317322 :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$"] [:db/cas 17592186317322 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5\\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5\\cos \\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5\\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5\\cos \\,\\left(3\\,\\left(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cos \\,\\left(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317323 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=5\\pi $: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))\\,k \\\\  & =(0)\\,i-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(5\\pi ) & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (0)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(5\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(5\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=5\\pi $: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(0)\\,i-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(5\\pi ) & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (0)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(5\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(5\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317324 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)\\left(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}\\right) \\\\  & =u(t-3)\\left(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317329 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317330 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317330 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos \\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\sin \\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),\n$$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)f\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\left[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos \\,\\left(2\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\sin \\,\\left(2\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot g(t),\n$$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317341 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-4x+(-2(x-2{)}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-4x-\\frac{2}{(x-2{)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =-4(3)-\\frac{2}{(3-2{)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-14.\n$$" "Let $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-4x+(-2(x-2{)}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-4x-\\frac{2}{(x-2{)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =-4(3)-\\frac{2}{(3-2{)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-14.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317342 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}-7\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6\\cdot 2x+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =8({3}^{3})-21({3}^{2})-12(3)+\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =216-189-36+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-9+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{80}{9}.\n$$" "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}-7\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6\\cdot 2x+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =8({3}^{3})-21({3}^{2})-12(3)+\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =216-189-36+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-9+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{80}{9}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317344 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2+1=A((-2)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,-1=5A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{5}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n1=A+2C\\,⟹\\,1=-\\frac{1}{5}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{s+2}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{3}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2+1=A((-2)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,-1=5A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{5}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n1=A+2C\\,⟹\\,1=-\\frac{1}{5}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{s+2}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}s+\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{{s}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{3}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317345 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+6L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}=\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+6Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+6),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+6)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}=\\frac{A}{s+6}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+3=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+6)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-6,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-6+3=A((-6)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,-3=45A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{15}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n3=9A+6C\\,⟹\\,3=-\\frac{3}{5}+6C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}{s+6}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{15}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+9}=-\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}+\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}} & ={e}^{-6t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}}+\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+6L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}=\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+6Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+6),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+6)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}=\\frac{A}{s+6}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+3=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+6)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-6,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-6+3=A((-6)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,-3=45A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{15}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n3=9A+6C\\,⟹\\,3=-\\frac{3}{5}+6C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}{s+6}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{15}s+\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{{s}^{2}+9}=-\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}+\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}} & ={e}^{-6t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}}+\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317346 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -6x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -6x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317348 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5{x}^{3}+3(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(-3)\\cdot 2{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =20{x}^{3}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5{x}^{3}+3(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(-3)\\cdot 2{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =20{x}^{3}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317354 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317355 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+8{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2}\\right) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}\\right) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+8{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317357 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{5-2{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0} \\\\ L{{e}^{at}} & =\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ (sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3})-2Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s(s+1)+15(s+1)-6s}{3s(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{5-2{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0} \\\\ L{{e}^{at}} & =\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ \\left(sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s(s+1)+15(s+1)-6s}{3s(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317370 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$" "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=\\left(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=5\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317372 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-17)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -14x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+