17592186317373 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{5}{4}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-15(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{5}{4}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5\\right) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-15(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317374 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ 12x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ 12x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317382 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186317382 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317389 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -2{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\left[-\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -2{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317390 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {\\left[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\left[-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot \\left(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317392 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317393 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317394 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint bet