  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left[\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\right]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2f/2fc15149d2097a1965e575e725b4dfb49401c1fe5bb1c5b3e43bf0f4f51743a1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320833 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{9\\cdot {3}^{2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/05/052e461c4f02a644254b4b9936b688c29afacd07432db7234bd71474dc9a076c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left[\\frac{9\\cdot {3}^{2x}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\right]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{3}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={3}^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{3}^{2x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/05/052e461c4f02a644254b4b9936b688c29afacd07432db7234bd71474dc9a076c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{3}^{2x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320836 :question/problem "![](images/33/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/33/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320836 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+9{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320837 :question/problem "![](images/da/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/da/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320837 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+25{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+25{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320838 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(2t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2t+1)^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t)^{2}+{((2t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}|t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(2t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2t+1)^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t)^{2}+{((2t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}|t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4}{2}+2\\right)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320840 :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =(5-1)^{3/2}-(1-1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1}\\right)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =(5-1)^{3/2}-(1-1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320841 :question/problem "![](images/19/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$" "![](images/19/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186320842 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{\\cos }^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{\\sin }^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/0b/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{\\cos }^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{\\sin }^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186320844 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =3+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3+2-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is $x=4.$" "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =3+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3+2-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is $x=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320845 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-2+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-2+1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=-1.$" "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-2+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-2+1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is $x=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320846 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =5+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =5+[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =5+(0-1)-(1+0) \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=3.$" "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =5+{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =5+[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =5+(0-1)-(1+0) \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320847 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(6) & =2+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6t-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{[3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =2+[3(6)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}]-[3(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =2+36-18 \\\\  & =2+18 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=20.$" "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(6) & =2+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6t-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\left[3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\right]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =2+\\left[3(6)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}\\right]-\\left[3(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}\\right] \\\\  & =2+36-18 \\\\  & =2+18 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=20.$"] [:db/cas 17592186320851 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(6t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(6t+1)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(6t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(6t+1)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}+1\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320853 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\left[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320857 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1-{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1-2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(3+\\frac{27}{3})-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1-{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1-2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left(t+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\left(3+\\frac{27}{3}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320858 :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+4t)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2})=4{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(4{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16+16t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+4t)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2}\\right)=4{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(4{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16+16t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320859 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/8f/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\right]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](images/8f/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320860 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0fc31d392bbaccf9a292553321bd60ccbdb2a02f3675543797f570c4fa0ef1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{3x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{3}a}}-1\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320862 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/fa/fac2eaeb6dfdda240d2bad7107f7a7e26ff688e89976437a95b99bc3f2ac288f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{3}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{{3}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{3}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/fa/fac2eaeb6dfdda240d2bad7107f7a7e26ff688e89976437a95b99bc3f2ac288f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{3}^{a}}-1\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320863 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{4}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\cdot {4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{4}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{4}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{4}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorn