tion $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/34/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{3u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left[\\frac{{e}^{3u}}{3}\\right]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/34/34abda46dc9fc2c6f14ce4f8460ba076a16004bb9141eb8074cdc08e0c12fd2f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{3/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320865 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to {0}^{-}$ and $u\\to 0$ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\frac{1}{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{-\\infty }{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{5u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{e}^{5u}}{5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{5x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{5x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/2a/2a24352ca6421b3aa61f3f29e3c19373cca011feafce263775a2f692f67ad36b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{5/x}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{5a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we substitute $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac