\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+4)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}\\right)(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}\\right)(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\left(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\left[\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)+1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+9)\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+9)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}}{4}(8-4+9)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}}{4}-\\frac{9e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320939 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+6\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+6)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-(5{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+10\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+10(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+5\\pi +0)-(-6+10) \\\\  & =5\\pi -4 \\\\  & =5\\pi -4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+6\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+6)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(5{x}^{2}+6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\left(5{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+6\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+10\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+10(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+5\\pi +0)-(-6+10) \\\\  & =5\\pi -4 \\\\  & =5\\pi -4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320940 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =((4{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+2+0-8)-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(4{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+2\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+2+0-8)-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186320941 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-2(-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\